    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d84">

                <pb facs="0113" n="106" sameAs="frus:frus1937v03/pg106"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">700.0011 Pacific/7: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-05-31T12:00:00+09:00">May 31, 1937—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received
                        May 31—7:12 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>140. Byas’<anchor n="20" xml:id="d84-f1"/> forthcoming despatch to the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">New York Times</hi> concerning a bilateral nonaggression
                    pact between Japan and the United <pb facs="0114" n="107" xml:id="pg107"/>
                    States is based on two recent interviews with Sato. Sato feels as I have
                    previously reported that the first essential requirement for the betterment of
                    Japan’s foreign relations is improved relations with China but he visualizes a
                    nonaggression pact with the United States as an important desideration for some
                    time in the future.</p>
                <p>Other comments on Japan’s attitude towards China and Mongolia are based on a
                    confidential interview which Byas has had with the Minister of War.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d84-f1" n="20" xml:id="d84-f1-content">
                <p>Hugh Byas, British correspondent in Japan of the <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Times</hi> (London) and the <hi rendition="#italic">New York
                    Times.</hi></p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
